.TH SMVIEW 1
.SH NAME
smview \- project a part into the lines of a drawing view
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smview
[
.B \-h
] [
.B \-c
.I depth
] [
.B \-t
.I chord
] [
.B \-u
.I material
]
.I view part.st
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Smview
writes one view of a part, in mm, x to the right and y up,
one record per line:
.PP
.RS
.nf
view VIEW PART
thick T
box X0 Y0 X1 Y1
fill [cut I], then m X Y, l X Y, a CX CY R A0 A1, z
line KIND VIS X0 Y0 X1 Y1 [by FEATURE]
arc KIND VIS CX CY R A0 A1 [by FEATURE]
poly KIND VIS N X0 Y0 ... XN-1 YN-1
hole X Y D [deep DEPTH] [csink DC ANGLE near|far]
     [insert TYPE near|far] [thread thru|DEPTH DESIGNATION...]
     [by FEATURE]
texture MATERIAL MARK CALLOUT
coat MATERIAL near|far N X0 Y0 ... XN-1 YN-1 [face]
radius R
bend X0 Y0 X1 Y1 ANGLE
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I View
is
.BR front ,
.BR back ,
.BR left ,
.BR right ,
.BR top ,
.B bottom
or
.BR flat .
The front view is seen along +y with z up, the top view along \-z with
y up, and the others in third-angle projection.
The flat view is the top view of the unfolded blank, with a center line
and a
.B bend
record for each bend (the angle positive toward the eye).
.PP
A fill begins a region of material, filled by the even-odd rule.
Arcs are counterclockwise, angles in degrees.
A record may have any number of fields.
.I Kind
is
.B e
(sharp edge),
.B i
(edge of a cut-out),
.B o
(rim of a hole, or arc partly hidden),
.B b
(edge or outline of a bend),
.B s
(seam where a bend leaves a flat face, or where two faces of a solid
meet smoothly) or
.B c
(center line);
.I vis
is
.B v
or
.BR h .
There is a hole record for each hole seen along its axis and not
covered, and a coat record for each coat on a slab whose side of the
coat looks at the eye, cut to the box of the slab, unless something
covers its middle.
Before the coats, a texture record gives each material of a coat that
has a texture
.RI ( plicad (7)):
its mark, T unless it has its own, and its callout.
A line, an arc or a hole names the feature of the part it comes from,
as the stream gives it, if it has one.
.B Radius
is the inside radius of the first bend.
.TP
.B \-h
Include hidden lines.
.PP
The stream holds one folded part, with the bodies of its inserts, or
solid parts: one, or the parts of an assembly in place
.RI ( place (1)),
which smview sees as solids, the folded ones too, as it does one
folded part with
.BR \-c .
Of solids, smview writes each edge, cut where it goes behind
material: a line as a line, a circle seen face on as an arc, and any
other curve as a poly record, a line through N points that keeps within
.I chord
mm of the curve (.01 without
.BR \-t ),
which no ordinate takes; the edges where two faces meet smoothly, as
a round meets its faces, are of the kind
.BR s ,
and those of a hole
.BR o .
The outlines of the cylinders and the cones whose axes lie across the
view are lines too; those of spheres, tori and pipes are left out, as
rounds show by their edges.
A hole record stands for each cylinder that bounds a hole seen along
its axis, at its end nearest the eye, unless something covers it:
with
.B deep
the depth of a blind hole from its mouth, with
.B csink
a cone of a countersink on its axis that widens from either end, near
at the end nearest the eye, and with
.B thread
the thread that the part gives the hole
.RI ( part (5)):
to its own depth, or that of a blind hole, or
.BR thru .
A coat record stands for each face of a texture that looks at the eye
and that the eye sees, in part at least, but one that a section takes
away, the polygon of its edges and
.BR face ,
after the texture record of its finish.
The textures of solids take marks for all the views alike: each its
own, or else the first letter from T on that no other has, so that no
two share one.
Where every face has a texture and there are two or more, that of
.B \-u
.IR material ,
or else the one of the most area, is the texture of all the faces that
the others leave: its texture record, first in every view, has the
mark \-, and its faces no coat records.
With
.B \-c
the view is a section: the solids cut by the plane at
.I depth
mm along the axis toward the eye, without what lies nearer; the faces
cut hide what lies behind them, and those of part I of the stream,
from 0, are a region
.BR "fill cut" " I,"
edged by lines of the kind e.
A solid view gives
.B thick
1.
.SH SEE ALSO
smdim(1), smdraw(1), stream(5)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A circle seen at an angle (an ellipse) cannot be written; the input is
refused, with the view and the place of the circle.
